# Excluded from the published plugin per `jbaruch/coding-policy: context-artifacts`
# ("Use `.tesslignore` to exclude build artifacts and CI files from the published
# plugin"). The plugin ships: `.tessl-plugin/plugin.json`, `README.md`,
# `CHANGELOG.md`, `rules/`, and `skills/` — including `skills/<name>/scripts/`,
# which hold the runtime scripts the skills execute. Never add an unanchored
# `scripts/` entry here: it matches at any depth and strips those
# (`jbaruch/nanoclaw-admin#435`).

# CI / build / test infrastructure
.github/
tests/
pyproject.toml
pyrightconfig.json
requirements-dev.txt

# Repo-internal decision records — history for maintainers, not agent context
docs/

# Python build artifacts
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/

# Repo hygiene / secrets
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.env
.env.local
# `.env.example` documents CI reviewer secrets for this repo's maintainers,
# not consumer configuration — the README never links it.
.env.example

# Tessl consumer-side install state. `tessl.json` is committed (it is the
# dependency manifest, not install state) but has no place in a consumer's
# checkout. Anchored so a consumer's own `tessl.json` is unaffected.
.tessl/
/tessl.json
.claude/
.agents/
.codex/
.gemini/
.mcp.json
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
